That was a porous part of the Allied line.

230
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:36,040
No one thought that Hitler would attack in the middle of winter in the midst of a thick forest with icy roads.

231
00:19:40,140 --> 00:19:47,140
Hitler attacked in force, stealing the element of surprise.

232
00:19:47,141 --> 00:19:54,140
The Allies scrambled to defend themselves, desperately trying to hold their positions

233
00:19:54,141 --> 00:19:57,140
with all divisions on all fronts.

234
00:19:57,141 --> 00:20:06,140
The Vibe 51st, a small elite paratrooper battalion of only 800 men, famed for their ability to

235
00:20:06,141 --> 00:20:11,820
to steal behind enemy lines were immediately sent to a small region near the German garrison

236
00:20:11,821 --> 00:20:16,300
of Rochelenbau on the most vulnerable position on the northern shoulder.

237
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:25,180
Their can-do attitude was legendary. They were just what the situation required.

238
00:20:25,980 --> 00:20:28,137
There was no time to arrange an airdrop.

239
00:20:28,138 --> 00:20:30,780
They marched towards the front in the heavy snow.

240
00:20:30,781 --> 00:20:38,120
We just come from the mountains where we had endured seven months of mountain fighting.

241
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:42,240
And we had just our summer uniforms.

242
00:20:42,960 --> 00:20:46,740
I found a couple of dead Americans and they took off their clothes and put on their clothes.

243
00:20:47,660 --> 00:20:49,540
We were bivouac in Barnes.

244
00:20:49,940 --> 00:20:53,820
And I remember that morning a sergeant from the 30th Division came up with a trailer

245
00:20:53,821 --> 00:20:57,540
and he had a 55-gallon drum full of hot water and sea rations.

246
00:20:57,541 --> 00:21:04,460
And that was about the 20th of December, and I don't recall having a hot meal after that.

247
00:21:05,700 --> 00:21:11,680
The 551 got across the mouth of the bulge just in an unbelievable fashion.

248
00:21:12,320 --> 00:21:14,720
They joined us just about Christmas Day.

249
00:21:16,460 --> 00:21:20,040
It would be the coldest Christmas the survivors would ever remember,

250
00:21:20,600 --> 00:21:23,060
with temperatures dropping to minus 7 degrees.

251
00:21:23,061 --> 00:21:31,300
There were portions of turkey brought up and served in the chow line out in the field.

252
00:21:31,560 --> 00:21:36,380
And I remember some of the guys would take a turkey leg and stick it up in the tree

253
00:21:36,381 --> 00:21:40,580
and figure, well, it's freezing weather so it's not going to go bad and I'll eat it tomorrow.

254
00:21:42,180 --> 00:21:47,260
At the front, the 551st was immediately sent in for critical probing missions

255
00:21:47,261 --> 00:21:49,480
to test the strength of the German defense.

256
00:21:49,481 --> 00:21:55,480
Speed was of the essence and the soldiers comfort was never an issue

257
00:21:55,481 --> 00:22:03,260
And some of the men that did have overcoats were ordered to strip them off and leave them so that we could move fast

258
00:22:03,261 --> 00:22:07,900
And eventually we paid for it with the frostbite cases

259
00:22:07,901 --> 00:22:17,680
The daring raids of the 551st fought from the 3rd of January onward were stunningly successful for such a small unit

260
00:22:17,681 --> 00:22:20,000
but impossibly brutal.

261
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:23,240
They were on a very long front,

262
00:22:23,360 --> 00:22:26,660
and every one of these engagements was a do-or-die engagement

263
00:22:26,661 --> 00:22:28,720
with very little help from tanks,

264
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:32,060
very little tank help from artillery, and no air support.

265
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:36,420
For three days and approximately three nights,

266
00:22:36,540 --> 00:22:38,500
we just pushed and pushed.

267
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:40,880
We attacked and attacked and attacked.

268
00:22:40,881 --> 00:22:43,880
We would gain probably 100, 200 yards,

269
00:22:44,920 --> 00:22:47,160
but we'd keep on pushing all the time,

270
00:22:47,161 --> 00:22:50,000
and we didn't have time to sleep.

271
00:22:50,220 --> 00:22:54,320
Perhaps we were just completely and utterly exhausted.

272
00:22:56,200 --> 00:22:59,300
They battled with every shred of strength they could muster,

273
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:02,280
even waging an historic bayonet charge,

274
00:23:02,700 --> 00:23:05,380
the second only such charge in World War II.

275
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:12,140
The darkest hour for the 551st would come on January 7, 1945.

276
00:23:13,420 --> 00:23:15,100
On the evening of the 6th,

277
00:23:15,101 --> 00:23:18,940
They were ordered to prepare to attack the occupied town of Rochelenwald,

278
00:23:19,700 --> 00:23:27,300
a heavily defended German garrison strategically located on the Germans' last escape route across the Somme River from the bulge.

279
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:35,300
The 551st leader, Lieutenant Colonel Wood Yurg, knew it was a suicide mission.

280
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:39,280
Over the radio, he begged to have the attack called off.

281
00:23:40,500 --> 00:23:43,340
This was witnessed by two of the 551st men.

282
00:23:43,341 --> 00:23:47,660
I'm not going anywhere till I get my men something to eat.

283
00:23:48,720 --> 00:23:54,460
And I think by that he was saying, eat, artillery, reinforcement, some clothing.

284
00:23:54,760 --> 00:23:56,600
They hadn't eaten for two or three days.

285
00:23:57,220 --> 00:23:59,820
And he was told, you're moving out at dawn.

286
00:24:01,380 --> 00:24:06,300
So this battalion, with practically no artillery, no tank support,

287
00:24:06,301 --> 00:24:14,960
We're looking up a steep, snow-covered slope, maybe 500 to 600 meters to the top of the hill.

288
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:17,040
They worked their way forward.

289
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:19,880
It took tremendous losses.

290
00:24:22,920 --> 00:24:27,040
Dick Durkee led A Company up a steep, narrow path towards the town.

291
00:24:27,860 --> 00:24:30,680
Two German machine guns were aimed straight at their advance.

292
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:33,160
We led the attack.

293
00:24:33,161 --> 00:24:39,540
and men probably knew that they were going to die

294
00:24:39,541 --> 00:24:42,120
and they just said, that's it.

295
00:24:43,960 --> 00:24:45,560
We've got to do it anyway.

296
00:24:47,260 --> 00:24:49,080
And I moved on up

297
00:24:49,081 --> 00:24:53,120
and me and the bazooka man knocked out one machine gun.

298
00:24:53,760 --> 00:24:55,340
Then we continued on up

299
00:24:55,341 --> 00:25:00,060
and got up to a wall that was just in front of the town.

300
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:02,920
And I said, now we got it, we got it made.

301
00:25:03,160 --> 00:25:08,480
And I looked back over to my rear, and I looked back, and I didn't see my men.

302
00:25:09,260 --> 00:25:12,940
I wondered, what the heck happened to the men? I thought they were following me.

303
00:25:13,700 --> 00:25:17,880
And then I saw my runner, Casanova, and I yelled to him to send up the men.

304
00:25:18,680 --> 00:25:23,160
And he yelled back and told me, he said, they're all dead, sir.

305
00:25:26,760 --> 00:25:32,920
That night, the two other companies of the 551st were able to overthrow the German stronghold.

306
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:40,160
Against tremendous odds, Rochelinval was liberated on January 7th.

307
00:25:40,161 --> 00:25:45,160
That action then showed Hitler that all of his troops had been pushed back west of the Somme,

308
00:25:45,161 --> 00:25:51,160
and it undoubtedly went into his decision to start the withdrawal.

309
00:25:51,161 --> 00:25:56,160
And we take great pride in having been a part of that.

310
00:25:56,161 --> 00:26:01,160
The 551st paid an enormous price for this exceptional victory.

311
00:26:01,161 --> 00:26:06,160
After such a severe mauling, the small battalion was disbanded.

312
00:26:06,161 --> 00:26:15,160
But their heroic efforts, fighting both the Germans and freezing conditions, were offered in sacrifice to stem Hitler's advance.

313
00:26:15,161 --> 00:26:22,160
An incredible spirit united the 551st to continue in the face of adversity.

314
00:26:22,161 --> 00:26:31,040
They did their job and they did it bravely because they were mentally conditioned to withstand those hardships.

315
00:26:31,460 --> 00:26:37,200
But it's awful wonderful for an officer to tell the men, you do this.

316
00:26:38,100 --> 00:26:44,580
And realizing that he knows he's going to die, chances are he's going to die to do it.

317
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:48,080
But they do it with a smile on their face.

318
00:26:48,081 --> 00:26:52,860
and that attitude you can only see an American soldier.

319
00:26:58,480 --> 00:27:03,640
After World War II, it would not be long before elite American soldiers

320
00:27:03,641 --> 00:27:08,640
would find themselves fighting for their lives in another winter battle of epic proportions.

321
00:27:08,641 --> 00:27:22,460
On September 15, 1950, during the Korean War, General MacArthur ordered an invasion of South Korea to eject the North Korean Army.

322
00:27:27,080 --> 00:27:27,780
The U.S.

323
00:27:27,781 --> 00:27:34,140
Marines launched an amphibious landing at Incheon that day and moved on triumphantly to retake Seoul.

324
00:27:34,141 --> 00:27:42,140
By early November, the 1st Marine Division and regimental combat teams from the Army's

325
00:27:42,141 --> 00:27:48,300
7th Infantry Division had crossed the 38th parallel and were advancing northwards, chasing

326
00:27:48,301 --> 00:27:55,540
the remnants of the North Korean Army towards the Yalu River and the Chinese border.

327
00:27:55,541 --> 00:27:59,140
They had no idea they were entering a trap.

328
00:27:59,141 --> 00:28:07,280
In fact, the Marines' only worry was the weather, which was turning bitterly cold the higher they advanced.

329
00:28:08,540 --> 00:28:16,440
On the 24th of November, the troops celebrated Thanksgiving in the freezing cold, which was dropping to minus 30 degrees.

330
00:28:17,580 --> 00:28:25,820
And we heard General MacArthur on the Jeep radio giving us Thanksgiving greetings from Tokyo, and he told us we'd all be home by Christmas.

331
00:28:25,821 --> 00:28:32,820
The companies at the head of the advance moved up to the edge of the Chosin Reservoir,

332
00:28:32,821 --> 00:28:35,820
which was now a giant expanse of ice.

333
00:28:35,821 --> 00:28:40,820
The first night there, I noticed some activity on the far ridge

334
00:28:40,821 --> 00:28:43,820
and I brought it to the attention of one of our officers.

335
00:28:43,821 --> 00:28:47,820
He said, we know about that. They're just some volunteers.

336
00:28:47,821 --> 00:28:53,820
Sometime way after dark, they attacked in force.

337
00:28:55,820 --> 00:29:02,820
The 15,000 marines were suddenly ambushed by a massed force of 60,000 Chinese infantrymen

338
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:09,940
flooding around them. Their orders were to annihilate the marines and allies down to

339
00:29:09,941 --> 00:29:11,740
the very last man.

340
00:29:11,741 --> 00:29:17,300
It took just a short time to figure out, no ammunition is going to come forward, no food

341
00:29:17,301 --> 00:29:21,020
is going to come forward, we can't get the wounded out, so what do we do?

342
00:29:21,021 --> 00:29:21,721
Pack it up,

343
00:29:21,722 --> 00:29:24,760
tighten ourselves into a ball, and move down to the next base camp.

344
00:29:27,440 --> 00:29:32,040
Thus began the historic ordeal of the breakout from the Chosin Reservoir.

345
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:35,440
It was a singular experience.

346
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,280
You were surrounded by other people doing the same thing,

347
00:29:39,020 --> 00:29:43,000
but you were in your own private war in one sense.

348
00:29:45,680 --> 00:29:49,220
Isolation and the savage cold forced the soldiers in on themselves.

349
00:29:49,221 --> 00:29:53,500
every second was a struggle for survival

350
00:29:53,501 --> 00:29:58,460
medics were overwhelmed by casualties

351
00:29:58,461 --> 00:30:03,100
when they were up on the line would carry the morphine cigarettes

352
00:30:03,101 --> 00:30:05,080
just a little one shot

353
00:30:05,081 --> 00:30:08,300
so they'd put these in their mouths and hold them between their teeth and their gums

354
00:30:08,301 --> 00:30:09,760
so they wouldn't freeze

355
00:30:09,761 --> 00:30:14,260
so when the wounded man needed them they could pull them out of their mouth and use them

356
00:30:14,261 --> 00:30:21,260
When the Chinese attacked, Red Merrill was shot in the upper arm.

357
00:30:21,261 --> 00:30:29,260
My rifle was in my left hand and my arm up like this to pull a trigger.

358
00:30:29,261 --> 00:30:38,260
And the bullet hit me from the rear, came out the front and caused a lot of severe nerve damage.

359
00:30:38,261 --> 00:30:43,260
Merrill lost circulation in his right hand and frostbite was quick to set in.

360
00:30:43,261 --> 00:30:43,961
set in.

361
00:30:43,962 --> 00:30:52,440
My fingers were all black from gangrene and I knew right then I was going to have some

362
00:30:52,441 --> 00:30:57,295
serious trouble. At the very least I'd lose my fingers.

363
00:30:57,296 --> 00:31:00,120
Many times the ice finished what

364
00:31:00,121 --> 00:31:03,200
the Chinese had started.

365
00:31:03,201 --> 00:31:12,440
The Marines managed to carve an airstrip out of a frozen piece of ground

366
00:31:12,441 --> 00:31:14,860
for planes to come in and evacuate the wounded.

367
00:31:17,120 --> 00:31:20,080
As they were taking off of the landing strip,

368
00:31:20,300 --> 00:31:23,800
the Chinese mortars and artillery were coming in trying to stop them.

369
00:31:24,020 --> 00:31:26,620
So you could see there weren't many people going out of there.

370
00:31:28,580 --> 00:31:33,060
Medics saw case after case of men who'd lost all feeling in their hands and feet.

371
00:31:34,640 --> 00:31:38,440
So he then took my hand and had me look away,

372
00:31:38,441 --> 00:31:44,480
and he poked it with my fingers and my toes with a needle or a pin or something like that.

373
00:31:44,700 --> 00:31:47,860
And I was supposed to tell him when it hurt.

374
00:31:48,580 --> 00:31:53,740
And it wasn't until he got kind of high up into my hand and high up into my foot when I could feel it.

375
00:31:55,600 --> 00:32:01,080
Almost every single Marine suffered from the flawed design of their rubberized boot, the shoe pack,

376
00:32:01,580 --> 00:32:03,620
which did not allow for adequate ventilation.

377
00:32:03,621 --> 00:32:09,620
Any sweat generated from movement would soon freeze between the toes.

378
00:32:09,621 --> 00:32:16,620
The boots were difficult to remove and frostbite would inevitably set in.

379
00:32:16,621 --> 00:32:18,771
It was impossible to eat.

380
00:32:18,772 --> 00:32:24,620
Soldiers either had no food at all or all rations were frozen solid.

381
00:32:24,621 --> 00:32:29,114
By and large, none of us ate for five to ten days.

382
00:32:29,115 --> 00:32:32,620
I lost 32 pounds in one month up there.

383
00:32:32,621 --> 00:32:37,460
It became so cold that their only thought was their very basic survival.

384
00:32:37,860 --> 00:32:43,320
If they would see any form of shelter, they didn't even consider the fact the enemy was over the next hill

385
00:32:43,321 --> 00:32:45,600
and might be in there shortly to annihilate them.

386
00:32:46,020 --> 00:32:48,400
And unfortunately, the statistics say that did happen.

387
00:32:50,960 --> 00:32:57,240
Occasionally, the clouds would break, allowing aircraft to move in to drop supplies and provide cover for their retreat.

388
00:32:57,241 --> 00:33:04,740
And this one night we all prayed to let the planes get through and all of a sudden it

389
00:33:04,741 --> 00:33:05,740
opens up.

390
00:33:05,741 --> 00:33:10,200
And we really were very grateful for their support.

391
00:33:10,201 --> 00:33:14,360
It turned the tide of the battle.

392
00:33:14,361 --> 00:33:19,400
Overcoming tremendous odds, the Marines managed to fight their way down to Hagaru and to the

393
00:33:19,401 --> 00:33:24,740
coast where they were evacuated by boat.

394
00:33:24,741 --> 00:33:32,000
The 15,000 allies that went up to Chosin suffered 12,000 casualties, including over 3,000 killed

395
00:33:32,001 --> 00:33:42,140
and 6,000 wounded, plus thousands of severe frostbite cases from the minus 30 degree temperatures.

396
00:33:42,141 --> 00:33:54,120
The Chinese suffered an estimated 43,500 casualties, including 28,000 killed and 15,000 wounded.

397
00:33:54,121 --> 00:34:00,580
The night the marines had prayed and the clouds parted, they had seen a very bright star.

398
00:34:00,581 --> 00:34:05,620
This Christmas star would become the emblem of the Survivors Association, known as the

399
00:34:05,621 --> 00:34:10,720
Chosin Few because so few men survived.

400
00:34:10,721 --> 00:34:15,360
To this day, veterans of the Chosin Breakout still remember the horror of the battle in

401
00:34:15,361 --> 00:34:17,900
the frozen mountains.

402
00:34:17,901 --> 00:34:21,440
Many have suffered amputations of hands or feet.

403
00:34:21,441 --> 00:34:25,100
have permanent nerve damage and still wear thick socks to bed.

404
00:34:27,400 --> 00:34:32,860
When I got back to the U.S., I couldn't stand to even have a sheet on top of me.

405
00:34:32,980 --> 00:34:34,040
I was sleeping at night.

406
00:34:34,720 --> 00:34:37,100
My body had adjusted to the cold.

407
00:34:38,880 --> 00:34:44,100
Their collective injuries have demonstrated just how cruel the effects of the cold can be.

408
00:34:45,200 --> 00:34:48,840
The government didn't really pay much attention to cold weather injuries

409
00:34:48,841 --> 00:34:53,340
until our Chosin few organization had a cold-weather committee

410
00:34:53,341 --> 00:34:57,560
who has worked very hard to change that.

411
00:34:59,180 --> 00:35:01,440
Great lessons were learned at Chosin.

412
00:35:02,120 --> 00:35:03,780
Better booths were essential,

413
00:35:04,360 --> 00:35:07,900
and soldiers desperately needed a proper cold-weather training program,

414
00:35:08,880 --> 00:35:13,540
especially if they were to fight in some of the coldest regions known to man.

415
00:35:16,260 --> 00:35:18,460
During the winter of 1915,

416
00:35:18,461 --> 00:35:24,560
Germany originated chemical warfare by firing shells charged with xyl bromide against the Russian army.

417
00:35:25,220 --> 00:35:30,980
Due to the harsh Polish winter, the xyl bromide froze solid in the shells and rendered them futile.

418
00:35:31,680 --> 00:35:33,280
Winter Warriors will be right back.

419
00:35:36,040 --> 00:35:42,760
As the Cold War set in in Europe, after World War II, the Iron Curtain divided east from west.

420
00:35:42,761 --> 00:35:49,740
On the northwestern border of the United States, a similar barrier fell through the Bering Strait,

421
00:35:50,760 --> 00:35:52,940
separating the Soviet Union from Alaska.

422
00:35:54,700 --> 00:36:01,700
Known as the Ice Curtain, it crossed a desolate expanse of water that remained frozen six months out of every year.

423
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:06,680
We were only a few miles from the Soviet Union,

424
00:36:07,300 --> 00:36:12,740
and perhaps this was a frontier now rather than Europe in the Cold War.

425
00:36:12,760 --> 00:36:16,660
the Russians had a vital interest in this part of the world.

426
00:36:19,120 --> 00:36:21,020
In this highly sensitive region,

427
00:36:21,480 --> 00:36:25,220
it was known that the Russians had intelligence bases to spy on the U.S.

428
00:36:25,940 --> 00:36:26,640
The U.S.

429
00:36:26,641 --> 00:36:31,080
was also very concerned that the Russians were operating submarines along the coastline.

430
00:36:34,060 --> 00:36:37,900
As in World War II, the unique skills of the Eskimo scouts

431
00:36:37,901 --> 00:36:42,340
and their intimate knowledge of the Arctic landscape were called upon to defend their borders.

432
00:36:42,760 --> 00:36:50,320
Generally, they had the same job as surveillance, but this time it was looking for lights for

433
00:36:50,321 --> 00:36:57,220
Russian submarines, anything unusual going overhead.

434
00:36:57,221 --> 00:37:02,880
And they did find flashlight batteries, Russian markings, some survival gear, part of an old

435
00:37:02,881 --> 00:37:04,880
rubber life raft.

436
00:37:04,881 --> 00:37:09,160
Operationally, they were very good at what they did.

437
00:37:09,161 --> 00:37:16,160
the Eskimo scouts up here during the Cold War, they were on the front lines.

438
00:37:16,161 --> 00:37:22,400
The Eskimo scouts began to help school both Army and Air Force troops in the essentials

439
00:37:22,401 --> 00:37:26,046
of Arctic survival and cold weather training.

440
00:37:26,047 --> 00:37:30,340
For soldiers new to the minus 40 degree temperatures,

441
00:37:30,341 --> 00:37:32,340
there was a lot to learn.

442
00:37:32,341 --> 00:37:37,340
Now a lot of those people came up from the lower 40s, didn't even know how to start a

443
00:37:37,341 --> 00:37:43,460
campfire and you always kept your matches inside your house never in your pocket

444
00:37:43,461 --> 00:37:49,580
could they fall through the ice or fall in the creek it is so much your matches are gone

445
00:37:49,581 --> 00:37:57,340
Alaskans could live off the land carrying dried food or hunting whatever animals they could find

446
00:37:57,341 --> 00:38:03,660
American troops new to Alaska had a hard time adapting to this new diet

447
00:38:07,340 --> 00:38:12,600
At the beginning of the Cold War, the Army also began staging winter war games in Alaska.

448
00:38:13,420 --> 00:38:16,740
For they knew that if they ever entered an armed conflict with Russia,

449
00:38:17,300 --> 00:38:19,780
it could be fought in extremely cold weather.

450
00:38:21,480 --> 00:38:25,780
American troops, equipment and tactics were tested against the Eskimo scouts.

451
00:38:27,480 --> 00:38:31,800
The perfect Arctic soldier, the scouts ran circles around the Army

452
00:38:31,801 --> 00:38:35,760
with their natural stealth and ability to blend into the background.

453
00:38:35,761 --> 00:38:42,760
They would come in, sometimes unseen, and leave little notes to the American soldiers.

454
00:38:42,761 --> 00:38:46,760
We were here, where were you? And they'd disappear in the mists again.

455
00:38:46,761 --> 00:38:50,760
Although the Eskimo scouts are no longer in service,

456
00:38:50,761 --> 00:38:57,760
most of their invaluable lessons have been incorporated into the current winter war games known as Northern Edge,

457
00:38:57,761 --> 00:38:59,760
that operate today in Alaska.

458
00:38:59,761 --> 00:39:08,080
Elite troops are committed to the same icy wastes, and their first lesson is to respect

459
00:39:08,081 --> 00:39:10,020
this merciless force of Mother Nature.

460
00:39:12,100 --> 00:39:17,260
With windchill, temperatures in the Arctic can drop as low as minus 80 degrees.

461
00:39:19,740 --> 00:39:25,820
Even rangers, who have run the gauntlet of endurance training, are not prepared for the cold.

462
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:27,720
There's a certain amount of macho.

463
00:39:27,820 --> 00:39:29,020
This is not going to be a big deal.

464
00:39:29,021 --> 00:39:30,400
I'm not worried about the cold weather.

465
00:39:30,720 --> 00:39:32,840
The day we landed was about 45 below.

466
00:39:33,520 --> 00:39:37,000
And as soon as they opened the back of the airplane and the cold air came in,

467
00:39:37,340 --> 00:39:38,800
you saw the expressions change.

468
00:39:41,020 --> 00:39:43,980
Drop a soldier off untrained, unequipped,

469
00:39:44,980 --> 00:39:48,200
in a cold weather environment in the Arctic.

470
00:39:48,580 --> 00:39:50,080
And you're talking about count the hours

471
00:39:50,081 --> 00:39:54,200
before you're going to come back and find him being a severe casualty.

472
00:39:54,201 --> 00:40:02,200
The bottom line is fighting in a true Arctic environment is a very, very difficult proposition

473
00:40:02,201 --> 00:40:03,763
across the board.

474
00:40:03,764 --> 00:40:10,200
And as a result of that, I think there's a very large impact of morale

475
00:40:10,201 --> 00:40:19,200
and soldier competence and confidence plays a much larger role in who will ultimately succeed in Arctic combat.

476
00:40:19,201 --> 00:40:27,280
back. They learned that layering clothing is of extreme importance. New fabrics and

477
00:40:27,281 --> 00:40:31,584
vapor barrier boots offer the best protection against the cold.

478
00:40:31,585 --> 00:40:33,360
They learned that mittens,

479
00:40:33,361 --> 00:40:36,265
if removed, will lead to instant frostbite.

480
00:40:36,266 --> 00:40:39,440
They learned how to operate weapons differently

481
00:40:39,441 --> 00:40:41,054
in sub-zero temperatures.

482
00:40:41,055 --> 00:40:45,120
A lot of them are taught as they put their cheek up to the butt

483
00:40:45,121 --> 00:40:49,220
stock of the weapon, in order to get the same sight picture, they put their nose against

484
00:40:49,221 --> 00:40:50,881
the charging handle of a weapon.

485
00:40:50,882 --> 00:40:54,360
In a co-weather environment, their nose almost becomes an immediate

486
00:40:54,361 --> 00:40:57,300
contact force-bite case.

487
00:40:57,301 --> 00:40:58,001
The U.S.

488
00:40:58,002 --> 00:41:05,200
Air Force runs an Arctic Survival School at Eilson Air Force Base, Alaska, teaching

489
00:41:05,201 --> 00:41:09,940
air crews how to hunker down in the ice and snow if they have to bail out over remote

490
00:41:09,941 --> 00:41:11,300
territory.

491
00:41:11,301 --> 00:41:16,480
I was actually told before I came here that if I ever had to punch out up here that I was a goner.

492
00:41:16,760 --> 00:41:20,880
But actually going through the course and really kind of knowing what I got at my disposal,

493
00:41:21,360 --> 00:41:23,440
I'm pretty confident that I could make it on the ground,

494
00:41:23,441 --> 00:41:27,900
set myself up for at least a day or two waiting for a pickup.

495
00:41:29,960 --> 00:41:35,400
Students learn many survival skills from experts, like laying out signals using their parachutes.

496
00:41:37,180 --> 00:41:39,840
V is going to stand out for our new help, pretty much.

497
00:41:39,841 --> 00:41:42,440
X, need medical assistance.

498
00:41:44,040 --> 00:41:48,740
They learned that building a snow shelter can mean the difference between life and death.

499
00:41:49,560 --> 00:41:53,560
Where it'll be minus 20 degrees outside, it's 20 degrees inside.

500
00:41:54,220 --> 00:41:57,580
And you can survive a long time, you know, even at 20 degrees,

501
00:41:57,680 --> 00:42:01,000
and you're outside of the area where frostbite's going to be more of a hazard.

502
00:42:01,300 --> 00:42:04,560
40 degree difference, just using snow as a shelter.

503
00:42:04,561 --> 00:42:09,560
They learn how to set snares to catch a meal if their rations run out.

504
00:42:09,561 --> 00:42:14,560
We give our students flagging tape so that way it sticks out so I can keep track.

505
00:42:14,561 --> 00:42:19,560
And you want to constantly make sure you check these things because nothing hurts the motivation and morale of survival.

506
00:42:19,561 --> 00:42:26,560
Then coming up to your snare, not being able to locate it, or coming up to it and find out another animal has already beat you to it and you fed it a free meal.

507
00:42:26,561 --> 00:42:27,261
meal.

508
00:42:27,262 --> 00:42:36,280
A lot has been learned since the early winter wars where Napoleon sacrificed his

509
00:42:36,281 --> 00:42:38,866
grand army to the Russian winter.

510
00:42:38,867 --> 00:42:42,940
Today's troops who are stationed in freezing regions

511
00:42:42,941 --> 00:42:45,704
around the globe are better prepared.

512
00:42:45,705 --> 00:42:49,440
But this knowledge has come at a tremendous price.

513
00:42:49,441 --> 00:42:53,600
previous winter battles decimated entire armies

514
00:42:53,601 --> 00:42:57,080
those soldiers who did triumph

515
00:42:57,081 --> 00:42:59,780
displayed extraordinary courage and endurance

516
00:42:59,781 --> 00:43:05,600
the survivors of the small 551st paratrooper battalion

517
00:43:05,601 --> 00:43:08,140
who fought so bravely at the Battle of the Bulge

518
00:43:08,141 --> 00:43:12,220
and whose history has been largely obscured for over 50 years

519
00:43:12,221 --> 00:43:15,560
were finally singled out for their exceptional service

520
00:43:15,561 --> 00:43:22,560
In May 2000, they were awarded a presidential unit citation for their bravery.

521
00:43:22,561 --> 00:43:30,560
This is only the second time since World War II that such a citation has ever been retroactively awarded.

522
00:43:34,560 --> 00:43:38,560
The Marines in Korea were also given a presidential unit citation

523
00:43:38,561 --> 00:43:42,560
directly after their heroic breakout from the Chosin Reservoir.

524
00:43:42,561 --> 00:43:43,261
of war.

525
00:43:43,262 --> 00:43:49,520
They were honored for decisively defeating seven enemy divisions, together with elements

526
00:43:49,521 --> 00:43:50,834
of three others.

527
00:43:50,835 --> 00:43:56,500
The doomed plight of these soldiers records some of the most grueling

528
00:43:56,501 --> 00:44:01,432
campaigns that were ever waged.

529
00:44:01,433 --> 00:44:10,500
As every winter warrior knows, no enemy is more merciless

530
00:44:10,501 --> 00:44:13,340
are more formidable than Mother Nature.

